"The surah announces that when the help of Allah and the conquest come, the Prophet will see people entering the religion of Allah in waves. Here, 'nasr' means victory against the enemy, and 'fath' means the conquest of lands such as the conquest of Mecca. Some commentators have stated that this help and conquest are not limited to Mecca alone, but point to all future conquests. Indeed, after the conquest of Mecca, Islam spread rapidly, and tribes embraced Islam in throngs.\n\nThe expression 'you saw the people' in the verse signifies seeing with the eye or knowing with the heart. The verb form 'entering' indicates that the entry was not yet complete, but would continue gradually. 'Fawj fawj' (fevc fevc), on the other hand, means in groups, troop by troop. This denotes that entering Islam was like a procession, and that this process, which began with the conquest of Mecca, continued until the passing of the Prophet.\n\nWhen these events took place, the Prophet was commanded to glorify his Lord with praise. Tasbih means declaring Allah free from every kind of deficiency, while hamd means praising Him. Here, tasbih and hamd are mentioned together, which signifies commemorating Allah's attributes of majesty (jalal) and bounty (ikram) together. That is, one must exalt Allah by remembering both His power and His grace.\n\nFurthermore, the Prophet was commanded to make istighfar, that is, to seek forgiveness from Allah. This was to be done both for himself and for his community (ummah). Istighfar entails regretting sins and resolving not to commit them again. Merely uttering words of forgiveness is not enough; heartfelt remorse and repentance (tawbah) are required. It is conveyed that Allah is ever-accepting of repentance.\n\nAt the end of the surah, it is narrated that these commands came at a time close to the passing of the Prophet. It is reported from Aisha that when the Prophet heard this surah, he began to make tasbih and istighfar abundantly. This shows that the religion had reached perfection and the Prophet's mission was completed. Drawing from this example, believers too should give thanks to Allah and seek forgiveness in moments of success and victory.\n\nAl-Alusi says: No matter how hard a servant strives, he cannot be free of shortcomings in fulfilling what is truly due to the majesty of the Worshipped. For this reason, istighfar has been prescribed following many acts of worship, such as seeking forgiveness three times after the obligatory prayer, the one who performs the night prayer (tahajjud) seeking forgiveness in the pre-dawn hours, and the pilgrim seeking forgiveness after the Hajj. It has been narrated that this command of istighfar pointed to the news of his death; with the religion reaching perfection, it was understood that the mission of calling to Islam was nearing its end.\n\nAt the beginning of the surah, with the promise of help and conquest, it was announced that the people would enter Islam in droves, after which tasbih, hamd, and istighfar were commanded. This command is a sign that the passing of the Prophet was near and that his mission would be completed. Indeed, this surah was revealed shortly before the passing of the Prophet, and Companions such as Abbas and Umar understood this.\n\nIn Razi's tafsir, it is noted that there are two paths for travelers of truth: The first is the path of those who say, 'I have not seen anything without seeing Allah after it'; and the second is the path of those who say, 'I have not seen anything without seeing Allah before it.' The second path is more perfect. This surah points to this superior path by mentioning first tasbih and hamd (directed toward the Creator), and then istighfar (directed toward the creation). Tasbih indicates the attributes of majesty, while tahmid indicates the attributes of beauty; istighfar, on the other hand, is to recognize the shortcomings of the self and seek pardon from Allah.\n\nFollowing the conquest and victory, the Prophet was given the choice between worldly blessings and meeting Allah, and he chose meeting Allah. This surah signifies that he would now engage in tasbih and hamd, leave the religion to his community, and meet his Lord. In the tenth year of the Hijrah, the Farewell Pilgrimage was performed, the final verses were revealed, and then the Prophet fell ill and passed away on Monday, the twelfth of Rabi' al-Awwal. His last words were: 'Nay, the highest companion (al-rafiq al-a'la).'\n\nAfter the passing of the Prophet, the conquests of Islam did not cease, and its enemies still failed to achieve their desires. To convey this, Surah al-Tabbat was revealed, reporting that the Prophet's uncle, Abu Lahab, was ruined by persisting in disbelief. This surah demonstrates the end of those who do not believe."